          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            is important for defining various structures in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+categories"} Freyd categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} dagger categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            cannot be defined via the usual definition. Instead, equality of sets is expressed through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            of sets, which, when applied to the definition of identity-on-objects functor, yields a bijective-on-objects functor.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            where categories are weak by default and thus the collection of objects do not form a set, the concept of a bijective-on-objects functor still makes sense for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functor"} strict functor
            by definition.
          %p
            Another motivation for bijective-on-objects functors is that they together with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functors"} full and faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functors"} strict functors
            form an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} orthogonal factorization system
            on
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/StrCat"} StrCat
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bo-ff+factorization+system"} bo-ff factorization system
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+kernel"} generalized kernel
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functor"} strict functor
              between two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is called
              = succeed "," do
                %strong bijective-on-objects
              = succeed "," do
                %strong bo
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi ob
                    %mo :
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{ob}: ob(A) \to ob(B)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi ob
                      %mo :
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{ob}: ob(A) \simeq ob(B)
          %h2#principle_of_equivalence Principle of equivalence
          %p
            To be more in accord with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
            on isomorphisms. However, from the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+systems"} factorization systems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            is nothing more or less than an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+Street"} R. Street
            in
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0303175"} Categorical and combinatorial aspects of descent theory
            proves
          %p
            Proposition. A functor is bijective on objects if and only if it exhibits its codomain as the (2-categorical)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+object"} codescent object
            of some simplicial category.
          %p
            This can be generalized to any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+2-category"} regular 2-category
          %h3#relation_to_equivalentonobjects_functors Relation to equivalent-on-objects functors
          %p
            Every bijective-on-objects functor between two strict categories is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalent-on-objects+functor"} equivalent-on-objects functor
            between two strict categories.
          %h3#relation_to_identityonobjects_functors Relation to identity-on-objects functors
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universe"} univalent universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity-on-objects+functor"} identity-on-objects functor
            due to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            and the fact that equivalences between sets are equivalent to bijections of sets:
          %p
            For sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:\mathcal{U}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B:\mathcal{U}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A =_\mathcal{U} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \simeq_\mathcal{U} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong_\mathcal{U} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoEquiv
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoEquiv}:A =_\mathcal{U} B \to A \simeq_\mathcal{U} B
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoBij
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoBij}:A =_\mathcal{U} B \to A \cong_\mathcal{U} B
          %p
            Since between equivalences between sets are equivalent to bijections between sets, there is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} EquivtoBij
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{EquivtoBij}:A \simeq_\mathcal{U} B \simeq A \cong_\mathcal{U} B
            which is an equivalence of types.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            is univalent,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoEquiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoEquiv}
            is an equivalence of types
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoEquiv
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoEquiv}:A =_\mathcal{U} B \simeq A \simeq_\mathcal{U} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoBij
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoBij}
            is also an equivalence of types
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} IdtoBij
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{IdtoBij}:A =_\mathcal{U} B \simeq A \cong_\mathcal{U} B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} BijtoId
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{BijtoId}:A \simeq_\mathcal{U} B \to A =_\mathcal{U} B
          %p Thus, in an univalent universe, bijective-on-objects functors and identity-on-objects functors are the same:
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong
                identity-on-objects
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: A\to B
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+universe"} univalent universe
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+functor"} strict functor
              with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} BijtoId
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %mi ob
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{BijtoId}(F_{ob}):ob(A) =_\mathcal{U} ob(B)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi ob
                    %mo :
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{ob}: ob(A) \to ob(B)
              is the underlying object function of the functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
